1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,872
Families are where the numbers get tangled.

2
00:00:02,952 --> 00:00:09,592
Four people are coming. Two of them dive. There is also a
six-year-old who will not be diving.

3
00:00:09,672 --> 00:00:15,448
It would be easy for a system to record six guests here, or to price
four divers.

4
00:00:15,528 --> 00:00:18,112
Look at the two numbers it keeps apart.

5
00:00:18,192 --> 00:00:22,816
Four guests is the booking. Two divers is what gets priced.

6
00:00:22,896 --> 00:00:32,872
The six-year-old is noted as a detail, not added on as another guest
— and because the children will not dive, the assistant asks who
exactly will.

7
00:00:32,952 --> 00:00:38,248
Rachel answers, and mentions the children's ages again along with
what she wants for them.

8
00:00:38,328 --> 00:00:43,000
The assistant does not lose the booking over it — the guest count
stays at four.

9
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:48,520
The booking is complete, and the guest count is still four — not
six, and not two.

10
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:55,168
The summary separates what is booked from what is assumed, so Rachel
can correct anything that looks wrong.

11
00:00:55,248 --> 00:01:01,528
And it ends the same way every time: no price, no promise, a person
follows up.

12
00:01:01,608 --> 00:01:08,776
That is the whole idea. The assistant handles the conversation. Your
team still makes every call about money.
